      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Q:",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Why do you think fewer young people read books today?",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A:",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "I'd say it's mainly because of the competition from smartphones and streaming. Young people have so many forms of entertainment that are faster and more interactive than a book. For example, a teenager can watch a whole series in one weekend, which gives instant satisfaction. Having said that, I don't think reading is disappearing — it's just changing. Many young people now read on screens or listen to audiobooks while commuting.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Q:",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "What makes a good teacher?",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "From my point of view, the most important quality is the ability to motivate students. Knowledge is essential, of course, but a teacher who is passionate can make even a difficult subject interesting. Take my old history teacher, for instance — she used to bring objects to class and tell stories, and suddenly everyone wanted to learn. So I'd say enthusiasm and good communication matter more than anything else.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
